  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing variances and preferring the higher-variance option. The individual, however, bases their decision on the probability of loss — specifically avoiding the 90% chance of loss in B. While both considerations involve aspects of risk, the conceptual focus differs: variance incorporates both the squared deviations and probabilities, whereas the individual only considers the likelihood of a negative outcome. The information considered by the individual (probability of loss) does not align with the information required for the variance-based reason (all outcomes and their squared deviations from the mean). Similarly, the justification (avoiding high probability of loss) does not match the rule of the decision reason (prefer higher variance). Although both lead to choosing A, the underlying reasoning processes are distinct. There is no evidence the individual computed or considered variance.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
